2 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0cbdba81-6911-563b-9935-b7f4c99e46ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1031 "Partitions of Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1033 240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049842 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "18160fcf-bc16-5973-b12d-9d8b5753934f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049855 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049855 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049855 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049855 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049855 #edb/ref 1031 "The Integers Modulo N" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049855 #edb/ref 1033 4823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049855 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "18f08686-8f9c-5129-8b91-3d25ddc36ebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1031 "The Conditional Definition of a Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1033 4425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050035 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "27c1f43a-d52e-5484-a2bb-f7742e7d634c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1031 "Indexed Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1033 55 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050088 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2c984580-e283-5888-86e7-f792567f1a8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1031 "Residue Classes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1033 2655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050597 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "574a92ea-12bd-55d4-a248-945298d922a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151846 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151846 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[5]_{8}?$\n\nI. $-3$\n\nII. $13$\n\nIII. $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41276052-e364-593e-9557-5d645f169a74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151846 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue class $[5]_{8}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $5$ modulo $8.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5]_{8} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=5+8k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={5+8k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,5-2\\cdot 8,\\,5-8,\\,5,\\,5+8,\\,5+2\\cdot 8,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-11,-3,5,13,21,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $13$ belong to $[5]_{8},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151847 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151847 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $3$ contain the integer $7?$\n\nI. $[4]_3$\n\nII. $[-1]_3$\n\nIII. $[1]_3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33da67df-113b-58e2-ad3e-a45d1568d75d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151847 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $7 \\in [a]_{3}$ if \n\n$$\n7 \\equiv a \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:3).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $7 \\in [4]_3$ because $7 \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $7 \\not\\in [-1]_3$ because $7 \\not\\equiv -1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $7 \\in [1]_3$ because $7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151848 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151848 #edb/ref 1071 "If $[a]_n$ denotes the residue class containing all integers congruent to $a$ modulo $n,$ then which of the following residue classes is equal to $[13]_3?$\n\nI. $[0]_3$\n\nII. $[1]_3$\n\nIII. $[2]_3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151848 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edf5a7d6-354d-534b-a8d3-5b29f2357a70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151848 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $[a]_{3} = [b]_{3}$ if \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:3).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[13]_{3}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $13$ modulo $3.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[13]_{3} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\cdot 3+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\cdot 0+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)} \\\\  & =[1]_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151850 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151850 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There are $4$ distinct residue classes modulo $8$\n\nII. $[5]_{3} \\cap [9]_{3} = \\emptyset$\n\nIII. $\\mathbb{Z} = [4]_{3} \\cup [5]_{3} \\cup [9]_{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151850 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8470a32-e938-5ae5-a732-688a6d62cefd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151850 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset$ if $a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\,  [n-1]_n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $8$ distinct residue classes modulo $8.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $5 \\not\\equiv 9 \\: (\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_3\\cap [9]_3= \\emptyset.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{3} & =[1+3\\cdot 1]_{3}=[1]_{3} \\\\ [5]_{3} & =[2+3\\cdot 1]_{3}=[2]_{3} \\\\ [9]_{3} & =[0+3\\cdot 3]_{3}=[0]_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{3}\\cup [5]_{3}\\cup [9]_{3} & =[1]_{3}\\cup [2]_{3}\\cup [0]_{3} \\\\  & =[0]_{3}\\cup [1]_{3}\\cup [2]_{3} \\\\  & =Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Elements Belonging to a Residue Class" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152097 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3d2f207a-8e99-5520-ab9f-266c8f54465b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Residue Classes Containing a Given Element" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152098 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9c1fe7ea-d91e-5440-b756-37a460a18f19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equivalent Residue Classes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152099 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6097ae19-85d5-5ca9-892d-889e843b3def" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152100 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d1a842b5-7256-59cd-b33f-d590bc315d84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1045 "Residue Classes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186152963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152339 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f5df7dc7-aff7-5140-9020-0f8e1d4c1cfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152792 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152792 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[6]_{9}?$\n\nI. $-12$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $24$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba3bb1ff-f2f2-5531-a68f-b27801ed7066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152792 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue class $[6]_{9}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $6$ modulo $9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[6]_{9} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=6+9k,\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={6+9k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,\\,6-2\\cdot 9,\\,6-9,\\,6,\\,6+9,\\,6+2\\cdot 9,\\,…} \\\\  & ={…,-12,-3,6,15,24,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-12$ and $24$ belong to $[6]_{9},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152963 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152963 #edb/ref 1061 156895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152963 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "257362f2-5f70-5a26-bc18-e59dfebb619c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152963 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152964 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152964 #edb/ref 1061 156896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152964 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92e60d57-6f02-556f-bc8c-58071a44c310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152964 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152965 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152965 #edb/ref 1061 156897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152965 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6e19ef8-a2b2-5b43-8bef-8b33d3e37a4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152965 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152966 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152966 #edb/ref 1061 156900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152966 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf05b2fd-4901-5647-b876-8df098ff47f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152966 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152967 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152967 #edb/ref 1061 156903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152967 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1e0e291-c1ef-5624-bf93-e964a6a92c9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152967 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152968 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152968 #edb/ref 1061 156904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152968 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c36a94c-bd92-5ac5-8b4d-2482b543bd82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152968 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152969 #edb/ref 1061 156907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152969 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2bda3d8-8738-5657-89ae-d2532c2a60cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152969 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153456 #edb/ref 1064 "Given an integer $a,$ the **residue class** (or **congruence class**) of $a$ modulo $n$ is the set containing all integers congruent to $a$ (modulo $n$). \n\nThe residue class of $a$ modulo $n,$ denoted $[a]_n,$ can be expressed using a conditional definition as follows:\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: | \\: x \\equiv a \\,  \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nPut another way, the residue class $[a]_n$ contains all integers that have the same remainder as $a$ when divided by $n.$\n\nFor example, the residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ contains all the even integers because they all have a remainder of zero when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{2} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)} \\\\  & ={2k\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-4,-2,0,2,4…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ contains all the odd integers because they all have a remainder of one when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1]_{2} & ={x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)} \\\\  & ={2k+1\\,|\\,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-3,-1,1,3,5…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that each element of $\\mathbb Z$ lies either in $[0]_2$ or in $[1]_2$ (but never both). Thus, these residue classes form a *partition* of the integers. We'll call this partition $\\mathbb Z_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_2 = \\big\\{[0]_2, [1]_2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can visualize this partition using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nSimilarly, the integers can also be partitioned into five residue classes modulo $5.$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_5 = \\big\\{[0]_5, [1]_5, [2]_5,[3]_5, [4]_5 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, each residue class contains all numbers that are congruent modulo $5.$ We can visualize this partition of $\\mathbb Z$ using the diagram below.\n\n![](images/27/270f63afd4a0022e9f31a48e5ea5f4e799822e8c4eb5813e6c5e616e316c02e1.png)\n\nA residue class is a specific type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.** We'll learn more about different types of equivalence classes soon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153456 #edb/ref 1065 9028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153456 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "26e8368d-0ec3-5b4d-8487-94376b8840d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153456 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153459 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's list some important properties of residue classes:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ *distinct* residue classes modulo $n.$\n\n- If $a \\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ then $[a]_n=[b]_n.$ In particular, this allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n\n- On the other hand, two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: \n\n$$\na \\not\\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n[a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset\n$$\n\n- The residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers. This means the integers can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct residue classes. \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n\n$$\n\nThe **integers modulo** $\\boldsymbol n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\,  [n-1]_n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $\\mathbb Z_n$ has some interesting properties. We'll learn more about those in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153459 #edb/ref 1065 9031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153459 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee8e604d-6ea0-5ad4-b5c7-a21b7d6c7a8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153459 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Residue Classes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153479 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's go back to our diagram for visualizing the residue classes modulo ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nAny given representation of a residue class is not unique.\n\nFor example, the integers congruent to zero modulo ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) are usually denoted ![](images/7e/7e03abb8339cac8bc90200b99c4de9c414e179036e7b7defca80f7388c9fdb7c.svg) In this case, ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) is the **representative** of the class:\n![](images/5f/5f298c564c0538edb6fc85abb585f5fef0b0bcff7fa704860794bb5681fb036d.svg)\n\nHowever, we can use *any* element of the class to represent the class. Here are some examples:\n\n![](images/38/383a513e20a70007e3d102dd4c8e9898550ac30514a3352842e1b9be8ad7601e.svg)\n\nWe can also use negative numbers as representatives of the class:\n\n![](images/01/014ec5b5f21d3b216343811bddb6d84c3a66c7b29baa09504d67cab71b83aea6.svg)\n\nWhat's important to understand is that *all of these sets are equal*:\n\n![](images/31/3155c29054f13a2b96de8be909ebd1ac6c98d50f64ea5d7a0acef3f1dcf48ac1.svg)\n\nThe same idea is true for ![](images/7a/7ac9fdeb690307bfa97c781ae9ace4d91bbf6f3b15cfe6dd7139225bdca44fc5.svg)\n\n![](images/cd/cd1101d39e228d23c551fcaa8ba377cd819661aaec6121706eab184658d2a854.svg)\n\nThe same idea applies to any residue class modulo ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg) For example:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4d211a3b3f6238f9e647585d62904af4fd263fb79eb94e45e9b51d8b62abde.svg)\n\nDespite this non-uniqueness, we usually choose the smallest nonnegative element of a residue class to represent the class." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153479 #edb/ref 1065 9215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153479 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "24c78443-01dd-5900-a2c6-3d43b549b1b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153479 #edb/ref 1067 "Representatives" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412807 #edb/ref 1080 "[5]_{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759